diff --git a/platform/docs/CHECKED_REGIONS.md b/platform/docs/CHECKED_REGIONS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1b26e9b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/docs/CHECKED_REGIONS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# Checked CPU regions for loading diagnostics
+
+I use `checked_region.h` and `src/checked_region.c` to load raw module images at
+fixed addresses and validate copies at 4 KiB boundaries. This interface is for
+trusted loader/probe code. It is not a completed guest CPU memory implementation.
+
+## Permissions
+
+The interface stores a permission for each logical 4 KiB page: none, read, or
+read/write. Native 16 KiB pages receive the union of their logical page rights.
+That preserves valid accesses to a writable subpage next to a read-only one.
+Every read/write operation through this API validates its entire span under the
+region's mutex before copying any bytes. A rejected cross-boundary write leaves
+both sides intact.
+
+Raw pointers bypass these checks on mixed native pages. Guest CPU loads/stores,
+atomics, SIMD helpers and host overrides must be integrated with the appropriate
+checks before this can serve a running game. Merely recording logical permissions
+does not enforce them on unchecked accesses. The address getter is for placement
+and reporting, not an unchecked write interface. External copy buffers must be
+trusted host buffers that do not alias managed regions.
+
+## Lifetime and failure
+
+The original create call uses eager backing. The sparse create call reserves
+address space without committing physical units. Commit changes logical 4 KiB
+state: reads of committed unbacked pages return zero; checked writes allocate
+64 KiB units. Raw pointers do not receive this service. Mirrors and GPU/CPU
+coherence remain separate work.
+
+Protection changes hold the same mutex as checked copies. A native failure rolls
+back logical permissions and attempts to restore native permissions. If rollback
+also fails, the region becomes poisoned and further checked accesses fail. All
+users must stop before destroying a region. No other code may change its mappings.
+
+## Evidence
+
+`make test-checked` uses the Linux kernel model. It checks fixed placement,
+collision rejection, exact checked-copy boundaries, native protection of a whole
+16 KiB read-only page, rollback, poison state, concurrent copies/protection changes,
+and release/reuse. The consumer's synthetic NSO tests check LZ4, file/image hashes
+and BSS integrity. Its MKWPROBE-002 application adds real-dump loading checks; a
+the user-reported MKWPROBE-002 console log passed all nine stages.
+
+## Checked atomic operations (experimental)
+
+The load/store/CAS API supports naturally aligned 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 byte
+operands. The same region mutex serializes them with checked copies and
+protection changes; seq-cst fences deliberately strengthen guest orderings.
+A CAS reports API validity separately from comparison success. On mismatch it
+updates the host expected buffer and leaves guest data intact. CAS requires RW
+even on mismatch; invalid calls do not modify output buffers or guest memory.
+
+These operations are linearizable among users of this API. They do not promise
+atomicity against unchecked pointers, external native atomics or GPU access.
+This is an experimental bring-up route, not the performance design for the
+complete game's guest memory. General memory, aliases and coherence remain open.
+The user-reported MKWPROBE-005 console log passed its SIMD, atomic and
+four-thread increment checks; MKWPROBE-006 also passed the shared CPU/HLE
+registry checks. These reports do not validate the newer sparse extensions.
+
+## Sparse reservations (host-model evidence only)
+
+`make test-checked-sparse` exercises 16 GiB and 64 GiB reservations, logical
+commit/decommit, zero-fill after recommit, dirty logical pages, privileged
+peek/poke, checked atomics, races and failure injection. No new sparse guest
+feature here has console evidence yet.
+
+A unit is allocated before its first checked write. `prepare_write` lets the
+consumer preflight every destination mapping before a multi-region write.
+Allocation or mapping failures poison that region; users must destroy it.
+Decommit returns pages to the reserved state, zeroes partial units while
+preserving neighbours, and releases wholly unused 64 KiB units. A native
+decommit failure also poisons the region.
+
+Privileged peek/poke may cross logical RO/NONE pages, never reserved pages.
+Peek stages its result; poke restores native permissions after copying. A
+failed restore poisons the region and may already have changed guest bytes.
+Dirty-page queries reset only entries returned, not unreported pages. Raw writes
+do not participate in this accounting.
diff --git a/platform/docs/DEMAND.md b/platform/docs/DEMAND.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..877e61f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/docs/DEMAND.md
@@ -0,0 +1,109 @@
+# Experimental demand-backed CPU regions
+
+I reserve a CPU region without backing all of it with direct memory. The first
+access to a 64 KiB unit allocates its physical backing and installs its view.
+`include/ps5platform/demand.h` is the interface and `src/demand.c` the implementation.
+This is a prototype checked with a Linux kernel model and cross-compilation,
+with the console evidence below. It is not a general memory implementation.
+
+## Fault path
+
+Creation allocates metadata for every unit and reserves two virtual windows: the
+public data window (with an optional guard) and a private staging window. The
+handler traverses an atomic registry and claims a unit with an atomic compare
+and exchange. It takes no pthread locks and makes no heap allocations. Registry
+removal waits for active readers before freeing metadata.
+
+The handler calls the exported direct-memory kernel allocation/mapping functions
+directly. It first maps a private alias and zeroes dirty backing there, then maps
+the public view. This prevents a racing reader from seeing reused memory before
+it is zeroed. The staging alias remains mapped until destruction: two virtual
+views use one physical unit, and no temporary hole can be taken by an allocator.
+Contending fault handlers wait briefly with nanosleep after a short spin, so a
+higher-priority waiter cannot monopolize the CPU of the mapping thread.
+Signal-context allocation/mapping passed the diagnostic run reported below.
+
+The automatic installer accepts a default SIGSEGV disposition or the exact
+SA_SIGINFO dispatcher explicitly registered by ps5_demand_bind_dispatcher.
+Binding verifies that this handler is already installed and never replaces it.
+Unrelated handlers are still refused. A shared dispatcher calls
+ps5_demand_access with the real read/write/execute kind before its crash report;
+execute, sealed writes, unowned addresses and backing failures are not handled.
+This integration avoids rejecting runtime tables/fibers after the application
+installs its own crash reporter, while preserving its fatal-fault path.
+Callers must not change a region's permissions or views behind
+this module's back. The region supports CPU read/write, not execute permissions.
+
+## Thread and stack lifetime
+
+Attach a thread before running on a demand stack. An existing alternate signal
+stack is borrowed; otherwise an eager guarded one is installed. Attachments nest.
+For a custom-stack pthread, back its startup unit before creating the pthread.
+The entry routine can then attach before executing callbacks with large frames.
+
+If the pthread owns the alternate stack, retire its final attachment at the end
+of the callback and release the returned token **after pthread_join**. Its TLS
+destructors still run on the demand stack and can fault after the callback returns.
+A thread leaving a fiber root on an ordinary stack can detach normally instead.
+No caller may access a region during its destruction.
+
+## Checks
+
+`make test-demand` builds `tests/test_demand.c` with `tests/demand_host_kernel.c`.
+The model gives newly allocated units dirty contents and uses memfd-backed shared
+views to test aliasing. The checks cover sparse backing of a 256 MiB region,
+zero-fill, concurrent first touches, registry churn while other regions fault,
+guards, NX, preserving a previously installed handler, and allocation/map failure
+cleanup. These tests do not execute the real PS5 kernel exports.
+
+The consuming MKW port additionally tests a 256 MiB fiber growing through several
+MiB, migrating between pthreads, and a destructor that grows a previously untouched
+stack after the worker callback has returned. Those are host tests too.
+
+## Console evidence
+
+I received the user's MKWPROBE-001 log from a PS5 whose reported firmware is 9.
+All five diagnostic stages passed. A 256 MiB region backed three units (192 KiB),
+and a 256 MiB fiber grew to 768 KiB of backing and migrated between pthreads.
+The log also reports successful thread/event/timeout/join and suspension/resumption
+checks. The evidence is the user's pasted log, not my own automated console capture.
+Concurrent first-touch races, registry churn, deliberate guard/NX faults and
+failure injection remain host-model checks; this run did not exercise them all.
+
+## Remaining limits
+
+This handles coarse CPU stack backing. It does not implement a guest's logical
+4 KiB permissions, write tracking, decommit semantics or GPU/CPU coherence. Those
+need their own mapping and synchronization design on the 16 KiB native pages.
+The extra virtual window and metadata have a cost; backed-byte accounting counts
+the demand units, not metadata, alternate signal stacks or other platform memory.
+
+## Sealed host tables (host-model evidence only)
+
+`ps5_demand_seal_readonly` is for host dispatch tables initialized once before
+publication. It protects backed units and publishes READ for future first-touch
+mappings; writes to an untouched hole are rejected too. The staging alias stays
+writable solely for zeroing newly allocated units. Seal waits for an in-flight
+map before protecting it. Failure requires destruction, not continued use.
+
+The host test seals a partially populated allocation, reads a fresh zero hole,
+and checks that writes to both populated and untouched units terminate with
+SIGSEGV. This extension has not been exercised on the console. It does not
+implement guest 4 KiB permissions or GPU coherence.
+
+## Shared runtime dispatcher regression
+
+The MKWBOOT-001 console log reached all three module loads/relocations, then
+failed to allocate its 74,973,184-byte dispatch table. The runtime had already
+installed a crash handler, which the automatic demand installer refused.
+The host test reproduces that rejection with the exact allocation size, binds
+an explicit dispatcher, and verifies sparse writes, zero holes, read-only
+sealing and rejection of writes/NX/unowned faults. Unbinding restores refusal
+of the still-installed custom handler. This is host evidence; the updated
+MKWBOOT-002 integration has not yet been exercised on the console.
+
+Owner-side ps5_demand_prepare initializes known ranges directly and records allocation/staging-map/data-map kernel failures in unit metadata. Results are published before FAILED; signal handling performs no logging. This is not a console fix proven for MKWBOOT-005.
+
+MKWBOOT-006 reports physical allocation success but staging MAP_FIXED failure 0x80020016 (fw9). On that error only, the private alias now uses the existing placement helper and is retained until region destruction. The public address, guards, zero-before-publication and protections remain enforced. Host tests cover successful fallback, rejection, zero fill, seal and cleanup; console validation pending.
+
+MKWBOOT-007 starts an additional guest thread, then rejects public MAP_FIXED at 0x820e50000 (stage3, 0x80020016). Both observed failures were between the low view area and the documented high area. Demand data/staging and alternate signal-stack reservations now request 0x1000000000 (64 GiB), as the Virtual space section of PROBE.md recommends for large arenas. Results below that bound are rejected and released. This is a placement correction awaiting a console run, not a claim that the entire intermediate interval is universally unmappable.
diff --git a/platform/include/ps5platform/biglock.h b/platform/include/ps5platform/biglock.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..38f9c66
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/include/ps5platform/biglock.h
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+/* Scalable reader/writer lock for the checked-memory hot paths. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
+ *
+ * Why: every guest load/store takes a registry gate and a region lock. With pthread_rwlock all threads bounce one
+ * cache line per acquisition (and every store/atomic took the lock exclusively). Here a reader increments a counter
+ * in one of 16 cache-line-sized stripes chosen from its stack address, so concurrent readers touch different lines.
+ * A writer excludes other writers with a mutex, raises a flag, and waits for every stripe to drain: writers are rare
+ * (map changes, protection, backing commits, 16-byte atomics) and pay the cost.
+ *
+ * Same semantics as a writer-preferring rwlock: readers overlap, a writer excludes everybody. A thread must not
+ * take the same lock for reading twice or upgrade read to write (as with pthread_rwlock). */
+#ifndef PS5PLATFORM_BIGLOCK_H
+#define PS5PLATFORM_BIGLOCK_H
+#include <pthread.h>
+#include <sched.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#define PS5_BIGLOCK_STRIPES 16u
+#define PS5_BIGLOCK_WRITER 0xffu
+typedef struct {
+   struct { _Alignas(64) int32_t n; } stripe[PS5_BIGLOCK_STRIPES];
+   _Alignas(64) int32_t writer;
+   pthread_mutex_t writers;
+   uint64_t since, queued;   /* time stamp counter when the current writer got the lock / asked for it (statistics) */
+   uintptr_t who;            /* the code that took it */
+} ps5_biglock;
+/* Statistics of who holds the lock exclusively and for how long (MKWBOOT-057): sched_yield from readers waiting for a writer
+ * was 9 percent of the CPU in the gameplay profile of 056. ps5_biglock_report() formats and clears them. */
+struct ps5_biglock_stat { uintptr_t who; uint64_t n, wait, hold, max; };
+extern struct ps5_biglock_stat ps5_biglock_tab[16];
+static inline void ps5_biglock_note(uintptr_t who, uint64_t wait, uint64_t hold)
+{
+   unsigned i = 0;
+   for (; i < 15; i++) {
+      uintptr_t w = __atomic_load_n(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].who, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+      if (w == who) break;
+      if (!w) { uintptr_t z = 0; if (__atomic_compare_exchange_n(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].who, &z, who, 0, __ATOMIC_RELAXED, __ATOMIC_RELAXED) || z == who) break; }
+   }
+   __atomic_fetch_add(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].n, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   __atomic_fetch_add(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].wait, wait, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   __atomic_fetch_add(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].hold, hold, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   uint64_t m = __atomic_load_n(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].max, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   while (hold > m && !__atomic_compare_exchange_n(&ps5_biglock_tab[i].max, &m, hold, 0, __ATOMIC_RELAXED, __ATOMIC_RELAXED)) {}
+}
+static inline int ps5_biglock_init(ps5_biglock *l)
+{
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < PS5_BIGLOCK_STRIPES; i++) l->stripe[i].n = 0;
+   l->writer = 0;
+   return pthread_mutex_init(&l->writers, NULL);
+}
+static inline void ps5_biglock_destroy(ps5_biglock *l) { pthread_mutex_destroy(&l->writers); }
+static inline void ps5_biglock_wait(unsigned *spins)
+{
+   if (++*spins < 256) __builtin_ia32_pause();
+   else sched_yield();
+}
+static inline unsigned ps5_biglock_rdlock(ps5_biglock *l)
+{
+   /* The stack address differs per thread (and per guest fiber stack), so concurrent readers spread over stripes. */
+   uintptr_t here = (uintptr_t)__builtin_frame_address(0);
+   unsigned i = (unsigned)((here >> 12) ^ (here >> 17) ^ (here >> 23)) % PS5_BIGLOCK_STRIPES, spins = 0;
+   for (;;) {
+      while (__atomic_load_n(&l->writer, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE)) ps5_biglock_wait(&spins);
+      __atomic_fetch_add(&l->stripe[i].n, 1, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+      if (!__atomic_load_n(&l->writer, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST)) return i;
+      __atomic_fetch_sub(&l->stripe[i].n, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+   }
+}
+static inline unsigned ps5_biglock_wrlock(ps5_biglock *l)
+{
+   unsigned spins = 0;
+   uint64_t asked = __builtin_ia32_rdtsc();
+   uintptr_t who = (uintptr_t)__builtin_return_address(0);
+   if (pthread_mutex_lock(&l->writers)) __builtin_trap();
+   __atomic_store_n(&l->writer, 1, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+   for (unsigned i = 0; i < PS5_BIGLOCK_STRIPES; i++)
+      while (__atomic_load_n(&l->stripe[i].n, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE)) ps5_biglock_wait(&spins);
+   l->queued = asked; l->since = __builtin_ia32_rdtsc(); l->who = who;
+   return PS5_BIGLOCK_WRITER;
+}
+static inline void ps5_biglock_unlock(ps5_biglock *l, unsigned token)
+{
+   if (token == PS5_BIGLOCK_WRITER) {
+      uint64_t now = __builtin_ia32_rdtsc(), since = l->since, queued = l->queued; uintptr_t who = l->who;
+      __atomic_store_n(&l->writer, 0, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+      ps5_biglock_note(who, since - queued, now - since);
+      if (pthread_mutex_unlock(&l->writers)) __builtin_trap();
+   } else __atomic_fetch_sub(&l->stripe[token].n, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+}
+#endif
diff --git a/platform/include/ps5platform/checked_region.h b/platform/include/ps5platform/checked_region.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1f50987
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/include/ps5platform/checked_region.h
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+/* Checked CPU regions for loader bring-up. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later */
+#ifndef PS5PLATFORM_CHECKED_REGION_H
+#define PS5PLATFORM_CHECKED_REGION_H
+#include <stddef.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C" {
+#endif
+enum { PS5_CHECKED_NONE = 0, PS5_CHECKED_READ = 1, PS5_CHECKED_RW = 3,
+       PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED = 4 };
+struct ps5_checked_region;
+/* The first kernel failure that poisoned any region (call, address, size, result code); empty if none did. */
+const char *ps5_checked_last_failure(void);
+/* Commits that got ENOMEM and were retried: how many commits, how many retries in all, and how many then succeeded. */
+void ps5_checked_commit_retries(uint64_t *events, uint64_t *attempts, uint64_t *recovered);
+/* Eager direct backing, fixed address aligned to 64 KiB, logical size in 4 KiB
+ * pages. Initial access is RW. No CPU code is executed from this region. */
+int ps5_checked_create(uintptr_t address, size_t bytes, struct ps5_checked_region **out);
+/* Large CPU-only reservation, initially uncommitted. Commit is logical only;
+ * reads of committed/unbacked pages return zeros, writes back 64 KiB units.
+ * Every access MUST use the checked API; raw faults are not serviced here. */
+int ps5_checked_create_sparse(uintptr_t address, size_t bytes, int watch, struct ps5_checked_region **out);
+int ps5_checked_commit(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, size_t bytes);
+int ps5_checked_decommit(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, size_t bytes);
+/* Preflight physical backing without changing guest bytes. Used by transactions
+ * crossing regions. Allocation/mapping failures poison the region, requiring
+ * destruction; no valid continued access is implied after such a failure. */
+int ps5_checked_prepare_write(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, size_t bytes);
+uint64_t ps5_checked_backed_bytes(struct ps5_checked_region *);
+/* Privileged copies through logical permissions, but never uncommitted pages.
+ * Host buffers remain trusted/nonaliasing. Writes participate in dirty tracking. */
+int ps5_checked_peek(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, void *, size_t bytes);
+int ps5_checked_poke(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, const void *, size_t bytes);
+/* Absolute addresses of reported dirty logical pages, ascending. Only reported
+ * entries are reset. UINT64_MAX for unwatched, poisoned or invalid queries. */
+uint64_t ps5_checked_written(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, size_t bytes,
+                              uint64_t *pages, size_t max, int reset);
+void ps5_checked_destroy(struct ps5_checked_region *region);
+/* destroy in two steps: retire releases the host range and makes every later access fail; free releases the metadata, once
+ * no reader can still hold the region pointer. */
+void ps5_checked_retire(struct ps5_checked_region *region);
+/* The per-page rights, the per-64 KiB backing flags (NULL for an eager region) and the per-page dirty bytes (NULL when not
+ * watched). The arrays live as long as the region; a poisoned or retired region has every page RESERVED. For runtime code that
+ * checks an access inline (ps5_cpu_memory.c). */
+unsigned char *ps5_checked_fast(const struct ps5_checked_region *region);   /* per 4 KiB: bit 0 direct read, bit 1 direct write allowed */
+void ps5_checked_arrays(const struct ps5_checked_region *region, unsigned char **rights, unsigned char **backed, unsigned char **dirty);
+void ps5_checked_free(struct ps5_checked_region *region);
+/* Every checked copy validates the whole span before touching it. Host buffers
+ * must be valid external buffers, not aliases of managed regions. */
+int ps5_checked_read(struct ps5_checked_region *region, size_t offset, void *host, size_t bytes);
+int ps5_checked_write(struct ps5_checked_region *region, size_t offset, const void *host, size_t bytes);
+/* Linearizable with all checked copies/protection changes, using the region lock
+ * and seq-cst fences. Width 1/2/4/8/16, naturally aligned. No interoperability
+ * guarantee with raw pointers, native atomics outside this API, or GPU accesses.
+ * CAS requires RW even on mismatch; failure updates expected, not guest data.
+ * Return value reports API validity; exchanged reports the CAS comparison. */
+int ps5_checked_atomic_load(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t, void *, size_t);
+int ps5_checked_atomic_store(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t, const void *, size_t);
+int ps5_checked_atomic_cas(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t, void *expected,
+                           const void *desired, size_t, int *exchanged);
+/* Up to 16 bytes, host buffer outside every guest region, caller holding a gate that excludes protect/commit/decommit/
+ * unmap (ps5_guest_memory.c's registry gate, shared). 1 done, 0 rejected, -1 (write) the unit has no backing yet. */
+int ps5_checked_read_gated(struct ps5_checked_region *region, size_t offset, void *host, size_t bytes);
+int ps5_checked_write_gated(struct ps5_checked_region *region, size_t offset, const void *host, size_t bytes);
+int ps5_checked_protect(struct ps5_checked_region *region, size_t offset, size_t bytes, int access);
+int ps5_checked_access(struct ps5_checked_region *region, size_t offset);
+int ps5_checked_run(struct ps5_checked_region *, size_t offset, size_t limit, size_t *end);
+size_t ps5_checked_mixed_pages(struct ps5_checked_region *region);
+/* Address reporting only. Native pages have the UNION of their logical page
+ * rights. Raw pointers bypass 4 KiB checks on mixed pages: this API must NOT
+ * replace a guest CPU memory backend without access instrumentation. */
+uintptr_t ps5_checked_address(const struct ps5_checked_region *region);
+int ps5_biglock_report(char *buf, size_t size, double ticks_per_ns);   /* writers of the locks since the last call, by caller */
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+}
+#endif
+#endif
diff --git a/platform/include/ps5platform/demand.h b/platform/include/ps5platform/demand.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4614352
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/include/ps5platform/demand.h
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+/* Experimental demand-backed CPU regions. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later */
+#ifndef PS5PLATFORM_DEMAND_H
+#define PS5PLATFORM_DEMAND_H
+#include <stddef.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <signal.h>
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C" {
+#endif
+struct ps5_demand_region;
+/* Sizes round up to 64 KiB. Guard bytes stay inaccessible. Installation refuses
+ * an existing SIGSEGV handler unless explicitly bound as a shared dispatcher.
+ * Metadata and both virtual windows are allocated here, never in the handler. */
+int ps5_demand_create(size_t bytes, size_t guard, struct ps5_demand_region **out);
+/* A shared crash dispatcher must be installed with SA_SIGINFO first, then
+ * explicitly bound. New regions preserve that exact handler; an unrelated
+ * handler still causes creation to fail. NULL removes the binding only. */
+typedef void (*ps5_demand_dispatcher)(int, siginfo_t *, void *);
+int ps5_demand_bind_dispatcher(ps5_demand_dispatcher dispatcher);
+enum { PS5_DEMAND_READ = 0, PS5_DEMAND_WRITE = 1, PS5_DEMAND_EXECUTE = 2 };
+/* Signal-safe service for the bound dispatcher. It must pass the real access
+ * kind: execute and writes to sealed tables are rejected, never retried. */
+int ps5_demand_access(uintptr_t address, unsigned access);
+void *ps5_demand_data(const struct ps5_demand_region *region);
+size_t ps5_demand_size(const struct ps5_demand_region *region);
+size_t ps5_demand_reserved(const struct ps5_demand_region *region);
+/* Seal runtime lookup tables after initialization, before publishing them.
+ * Future read faults map read-only too; writes remain fatal. On failure the
+ * region must be destroyed rather than used with partial protection. */
+int ps5_demand_seal_readonly(struct ps5_demand_region *region);
+/* No users may access a region while it is destroyed. Other regions may fault. */
+void ps5_demand_destroy(struct ps5_demand_region *region);
+/* Signal-side service: no heap allocation or pthread locks. Uses exported
+ * direct-memory kernel operations. MKWPROBE-001 passed on a user's PS5, firmware
+ * reported as 9; see docs/DEMAND.md for scope. Returns 0 for an unowned address
+ * or a backing failure. */
+int ps5_demand_fault(uintptr_t address);
+/* Owner-side initialization, before publishing a stack. 0 = success; failure
+ * retains the native kernel result and phase, without logging in the handler.
+ * Caller keeps region alive. Offsets/sizes must stay inside the data window. */
+struct ps5_demand_failure { int stage, result; uintptr_t data, staging; int64_t physical; };
+enum { PS5_DEMAND_ALLOCATE = 1, PS5_DEMAND_MAP_STAGING = 2, PS5_DEMAND_MAP_DATA = 3 };
+int ps5_demand_prepare(struct ps5_demand_region *region, size_t offset, size_t bytes,
+                       struct ps5_demand_failure *failure);
+uint64_t ps5_demand_backed_bytes(void);
+/* Attach before running on a demand stack, detach after leaving it. Nestable.
+ * An existing alternate stack is borrowed; otherwise an eager one is installed. */
+int ps5_demand_thread_attach(void);
+void ps5_demand_thread_detach(void);
+/* For custom-stack pthreads: keep the alternate stack valid through pthread
+ * destructors. Retire the final attachment on the worker, release its token
+ * only after pthread_join confirms the worker has ended. */
+int ps5_demand_thread_retire(void **token);
+void ps5_demand_thread_release(void *token);
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+}
+#endif
+#endif
diff --git a/platform/src/checked_region.c b/platform/src/checked_region.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d2bb8c2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/src/checked_region.c
@@ -0,0 +1,754 @@
+/* Checked loader regions; not an unchecked guest memory backend.
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later */
+#include "ps5platform/checked_region.h"
+#include "ps5platform/biglock.h"
+#include "ps5platform/kernel.h"
+#include "ps5platform/shm.h"
+#include <pthread.h>
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include <time.h>
+#include <string.h>
+
+// Locking: ps5_biglock (include/ps5platform/biglock.h). Readers overlap; protection, backing, 16-byte atomics and
+// metadata changes are exclusive. Plain data stores and atomics up to 8 bytes run shared on host atomics.
+// The first failure that poisoned a region, for fatal reports: which kernel call, where, and its result code.
+static char last_failure[384];
+static int last_failure_set;
+// What still works right after the first failure, to tell a size limit from a count limit: a small allocation, a larger
+// one, and mapping the small one anywhere. Every probe is undone at once. Runs once.
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+static void probe_after_failure(char *out, size_t cap)
+{
+   int64_t total = sceKernelGetDirectMemorySize(), at = -1, big = -1, small = -1;
+   size_t largest = 0;
+   int32_t a64 = sceKernelAllocateDirectMemory(0, total, 0x10000, 0x10000, PS5_KERNEL_DIRECT_TYPE_CPU, &at);
+   int32_t a2m = sceKernelAllocateDirectMemory(0, total, 0x200000, 0x200000, PS5_KERNEL_DIRECT_TYPE_CPU, &big);
+   int32_t a16 = sceKernelAllocateDirectMemory(0, total, 0x4000, 0x4000, PS5_KERNEL_DIRECT_TYPE_CPU, &small);
+   int32_t map = -1;
+   if (a64 == 0) {
+      void *where = NULL;
+      map = sceKernelMapDirectMemory(&where, 0x10000, PS5_KERNEL_PROT_CPU_READ, 0, at, 0x4000);
+      if (map == 0) sceKernelMunmap(where, 0x10000);
+   }
+   if (a64 == 0) sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(at, 0x10000);
+   if (a2m == 0) sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(big, 0x200000);
+   if (a16 == 0) sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(small, 0x4000);
+   int64_t start = 0;
+   if (sceKernelAvailableDirectMemorySize(0, total, 0, &start, &largest)) largest = 0;
+   snprintf(out, cap, "; probe: alloc 64K=%#x 2M=%#x 16K=%#x, map 64K anywhere=%#x, largest free %llu MiB of %llu",
+            (unsigned)a64, (unsigned)a2m, (unsigned)a16, (unsigned)map, (unsigned long long)(largest >> 20),
+            (unsigned long long)(total >> 20));
+}
+#else
+static void probe_after_failure(char *out, size_t cap) { (void)out; (void)cap; }
+#endif
+static void note_failure(const char *what, uintptr_t address, size_t bytes, int result)
+{
+   if (__atomic_exchange_n(&last_failure_set, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL)) return;
+   int n = snprintf(last_failure, sizeof last_failure, "first failure: %s at %#llx +%#llx = %#x",
+                    what, (unsigned long long)address, (unsigned long long)bytes, (unsigned)result);
+   if (n > 0 && (size_t)n < sizeof last_failure) probe_after_failure(last_failure + n, sizeof last_failure - (size_t)n);
+}
+const char *ps5_checked_last_failure(void) { return last_failure; }
+static uint64_t retry_events, retry_attempts, retry_recovered;
+void ps5_checked_commit_retries(uint64_t *events, uint64_t *attempts, uint64_t *recovered)
+{
+   *events = __atomic_load_n(&retry_events, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   *attempts = __atomic_load_n(&retry_attempts, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   *recovered = __atomic_load_n(&retry_recovered, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+}
+#define LOGICAL ((size_t)0x1000)
+#define NATIVE ((size_t)0x4000)
+#define UNIT ((size_t)0x10000)
+struct ps5_checked_region {
+   uintptr_t base;
+   size_t bytes, reserved, pages;
+   unsigned char *rights;
+   unsigned char *backed, *dirty;
+   /* MKWBOOT-058: per 4 KiB, what the game code may do directly without asking: bit 0 read, bit 1 write; 0 when the page is
+    * reserved, its 64 KiB is not backed or the region is poisoned. The generated game code reads it inline (runtime cpu.h),
+    * so it only ever grants after the host mapping allows the access and drops before the mapping stops allowing it.
+    * Lives right after rights in the same allocation. */
+   unsigned char *fast;
+   ps5_biglock lock;
+   int poisoned;
+   int retired;
+};
+static int span(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   return !r->poisoned && offset <= r->bytes && bytes <= r->bytes - offset;
+}
+// A poisoned or retired region must refuse every access, including the inline fast path of the runtime that reads the rights
+// array directly (ps5_cpu_memory.c): every page becomes RESERVED, which has neither the read nor the write bit.
+static void invalidate(struct ps5_checked_region *r)
+{
+   __atomic_store_n(&r->poisoned, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+   if (r->fast) memset(r->fast, 0, r->pages);
+   if (r->rights) memset(r->rights, PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED, r->pages);
+}
+/* fast[] from rights[] and backed[] for pages [first, last): call after a change that grants (mapping already allows it) */
+static void fast_refresh(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t first, size_t last)
+{
+   if (last > r->pages) last = r->pages;
+   for (size_t p = first; p < last; p++) {
+      unsigned char v = r->poisoned || (r->backed && !r->backed[p * LOGICAL / UNIT]) ? 0 : (unsigned char)(r->rights[p] & 3);
+      if (r->rights[p] == PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED) v = 0;
+      __atomic_store_n(&r->fast[p], v, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+   }
+}
+/* before a change that may take rights away: keep only what both the old and the new rights allow */
+static void fast_drop(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t first, size_t last, unsigned char keep)
+{
+   if (last > r->pages) last = r->pages;
+   for (size_t p = first; p < last; p++) __atomic_store_n(&r->fast[p], (unsigned char)(r->fast[p] & keep), __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+}
+static int aggregate(const struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t native_page)
+{
+   int access = 0;
+   for (size_t i = native_page * 4; i < native_page * 4 + 4 && i < r->pages; i++)
+      if (r->rights[i] != PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED) access |= r->rights[i];
+   return access;
+}
+/* MKWBOOT-063: one sceKernelMprotect per run of contiguous backed native pages with the same protection, not one per
+ * 16 KiB page: ps5_checked_protect held the exclusive lock 385 ms of every 5 s in a race (MKWBOOT-062), up to 6 ms a call. */
+static int apply(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t first, size_t last)
+{
+   size_t page = first;
+   while (page < last) {
+      if (r->backed && !r->backed[page * NATIVE / UNIT]) { page++; continue; }
+      int prot = aggregate(r, page);
+      size_t end = page + 1;
+      while (end < last && !(r->backed && !r->backed[end * NATIVE / UNIT]) && aggregate(r, end) == prot) end++;
+      int rc = sceKernelMprotect((void *)(r->base + page * NATIVE), (end - page) * NATIVE, prot);
+      if (rc) { note_failure("sceKernelMprotect", r->base + page * NATIVE, (end - page) * NATIVE, rc); return 0; }
+      page = end;
+   }
+   return 1;
+}
+static int create_region(uintptr_t address, size_t bytes, int sparse, int watch, struct ps5_checked_region **out)
+{
+   if (!out) return 0;
+   *out = NULL;
+   if (!address || address % UNIT || !bytes || bytes % LOGICAL || bytes > SIZE_MAX - UNIT) return 0;
+   size_t reserved = (bytes + UNIT - 1) & ~(UNIT - 1);
+   if (address > UINTPTR_MAX - reserved) return 0;
+   struct ps5_checked_region *r = calloc(1, sizeof(*r));
+   if (!r) return 0;
+   r->rights = malloc(2 * (bytes / LOGICAL));
+   if (!r->rights) { free(r); return 0; }
+   r->fast = r->rights + bytes / LOGICAL;
+   if (sparse) r->backed = calloc(reserved / UNIT, 1);
+   if (watch) r->dirty = calloc(bytes / LOGICAL, 1);
+   if ((sparse && !r->backed) || (watch && !r->dirty)) {
+      free(r->dirty); free(r->backed); free(r->rights); free(r); return 0;
+   }
+   if (ps5_biglock_init(&r->lock)) { free(r->dirty); free(r->backed); free(r->rights); free(r); return 0; }
+   if (ps5_vrange_reserve_at((void *)address, reserved)) goto fail;
+   if (!sparse && ps5_vrange_commit((void *)address, bytes, PS5_SHM_READ | PS5_SHM_WRITE)) {
+      ps5_vrange_release((void *)address, reserved);
+      goto fail;
+   }
+   r->base = address; r->bytes = bytes; r->reserved = reserved; r->pages = bytes / LOGICAL;
+   memset(r->rights, sparse ? PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED : PS5_CHECKED_RW, r->pages);
+   memset(r->fast, sparse ? 0 : 3, r->pages);
+   *out = r;
+   return 1;
+fail:
+   ps5_biglock_destroy(&r->lock);
+   free(r->dirty); free(r->backed); free(r->rights); free(r);
+   return 0;
+}
+int ps5_checked_create(uintptr_t address, size_t bytes, struct ps5_checked_region **out)
+{ return create_region(address, bytes, 0, 0, out); }
+int ps5_checked_create_sparse(uintptr_t address, size_t bytes, int watch, struct ps5_checked_region **out)
+{ return create_region(address, bytes, 1, watch, out); }
+// Retire, then free, so a lock-free reader that still holds the region (ps5_guest_memory.c, MKWBOOT-042) finds poisoned
+// metadata instead of freed memory: the retired region refuses every access and the host range is released at once; the
+// metadata is freed later by ps5_checked_free, once no reader can still hold it.
+void ps5_checked_arrays(const struct ps5_checked_region *r, unsigned char **rights, unsigned char **backed, unsigned char **dirty)
+{
+   *rights = r->rights; *backed = r->backed; *dirty = r->dirty;
+}
+unsigned char *ps5_checked_fast(const struct ps5_checked_region *r) { return r->fast; }
+void ps5_checked_retire(struct ps5_checked_region *r)
+{
+   if (!r || __atomic_exchange_n(&r->retired, 1, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL)) return;
+   invalidate(r);
+   if (ps5_vrange_release((void *)r->base, r->reserved)) __builtin_trap();
+}
+void ps5_checked_free(struct ps5_checked_region *r)
+{
+   if (!r) return;
+   ps5_biglock_destroy(&r->lock);
+   free(r->dirty); free(r->backed); free(r->rights); free(r);
+}
+void ps5_checked_destroy(struct ps5_checked_region *r)
+{
+   if (!r) return;
+   // The caller has stopped all users before destruction.
+   ps5_checked_retire(r);
+   ps5_checked_free(r);
+}
+static int committed(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!span(r, offset, bytes)) return 0;
+   if (!bytes) return 1;
+   for (size_t p = offset / LOGICAL; p <= (offset + bytes - 1) / LOGICAL; p++)
+      if (r->rights[p] == PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED) return 0;
+   return 1;
+}
+static int ensure_backing_units(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes);
+/* MKWBOOT-073: the units still unbacked in [offset, offset + bytes) are committed a run at a time: one ps5_vrange_commit and
+ * one protection pass for each run of consecutive unbacked 64 KiB units, not one of each per unit. The game streams assets
+ * in a race (romfs reads and zstd output into fresh guest memory) and every unit cost several system calls under the
+ * exclusive lock while every other thread waited (the 0.5-2 s stalls of 072). A run whose commit fails goes unit by unit
+ * through the path below, with its ENOMEM retries and poison, exactly as before. */
+static int ensure_backing(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!r->backed || !bytes) return 1;
+   size_t u = offset / UNIT, last = (offset + bytes - 1) / UNIT;
+   while (u <= last) {
+      if (r->backed[u]) { u++; continue; }
+      size_t end = u + 1;
+      while (end <= last && !r->backed[end]) end++;
+      if (end - u > 1 && !ps5_vrange_commit((void *)(r->base + u * UNIT), (end - u) * UNIT, PS5_SHM_READ | PS5_SHM_WRITE)) {
+         for (size_t k = u; k < end; k++) __atomic_store_n(&r->backed[k], 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+         if (!apply(r, u * UNIT / NATIVE, end * UNIT / NATIVE)) { invalidate(r); return 0; }
+         fast_refresh(r, u * UNIT / LOGICAL, end * UNIT / LOGICAL);
+      } else if (!ensure_backing_units(r, u * UNIT, (end - u) * UNIT)) return 0;
+      u = end;
+   }
+   return 1;
+}
+static int ensure_backing_units(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!r->backed || !bytes) return 1;
+   for (size_t u = offset / UNIT; u <= (offset + bytes - 1) / UNIT; u++) {
+      if (r->backed[u]) continue;
+      int rc = ps5_vrange_commit((void *)(r->base + u * UNIT), UNIT, PS5_SHM_READ | PS5_SHM_WRITE);
+      // MKWBOOT-033: ENOMEM (0x8002000c) from the console's fixed-address map came with 6+ GiB free and everything else
+      // still granted a moment later (MKWBOOT-029..032), so it may be transient. Retry for up to ~0.4 s before poisoning
+      // the region, and count it: ps5_checked_commit_retries() tells whether this ever helped.
+      if (rc == (int)0x8002000c) {
+         for (int attempt = 0; rc == (int)0x8002000c && attempt < 200; attempt++) {
+            struct timespec pause = {0, 2000000};
+            nanosleep(&pause, NULL);
+            rc = ps5_vrange_commit((void *)(r->base + u * UNIT), UNIT, PS5_SHM_READ | PS5_SHM_WRITE);
+            __atomic_add_fetch(&retry_attempts, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+         }
+         __atomic_add_fetch(&retry_events, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+         if (!rc) __atomic_add_fetch(&retry_recovered, 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+      }
+      if (rc) {
+         note_failure("ps5_vrange_commit", r->base + u * UNIT, UNIT, rc);
+         invalidate(r); return 0;
+      }
+      __atomic_store_n(&r->backed[u], 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);   // gated readers (below) take no lock: they see the unit only once it is mapped
+      if (!apply(r, u * UNIT / NATIVE, (u + 1) * UNIT / NATIVE)) { invalidate(r); return 0; }
+      fast_refresh(r, u * UNIT / LOGICAL, (u + 1) * UNIT / LOGICAL);
+   }
+   return 1;
+}
+// Naturally aligned 1/2/4/8-byte accesses are single host loads/stores, as on the console: a plain store racing an
+// atomic never tears. Region bases are 64 KiB aligned, so an aligned offset is an aligned host address.
+static int small_access(size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   return (bytes == 1 || bytes == 2 || bytes == 4 || bytes == 8) && !(offset % bytes);
+}
+typedef unsigned char vec16 __attribute__((vector_size(16)));
+// Aligned 16-byte accesses (guest vector loads/stores) are one host vector access: a larger write stays exclusive, so
+// a reader never sees part of it (test_ps5_memory_readers), and a 16-byte one is a single instruction either way.
+static int vector_access(size_t offset, size_t bytes) { return bytes == 16 && !(offset % 16); }
+static int backed_all(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!r->backed || !bytes) return 1;
+   for (size_t u = offset / UNIT; u <= (offset + bytes - 1) / UNIT; u++) if (!r->backed[u]) return 0;
+   return 1;
+}
+static void load_small(const void *src, void *out, size_t n, int order)
+{
+   switch (n) {
+   case 1: { uint8_t v = __atomic_load_n((const uint8_t *)src, order); memcpy(out, &v, 1); break; }
+   case 2: { uint16_t v = __atomic_load_n((const uint16_t *)src, order); memcpy(out, &v, 2); break; }
+   case 4: { uint32_t v = __atomic_load_n((const uint32_t *)src, order); memcpy(out, &v, 4); break; }
+   default: { uint64_t v = __atomic_load_n((const uint64_t *)src, order); memcpy(out, &v, 8); break; }
+   }
+}
+static void store_small(void *dst, const void *in, size_t n, int order)
+{
+   switch (n) {
+   case 1: { uint8_t v; memcpy(&v, in, 1); __atomic_store_n((uint8_t *)dst, v, order); break; }
+   case 2: { uint16_t v; memcpy(&v, in, 2); __atomic_store_n((uint16_t *)dst, v, order); break; }
+   case 4: { uint32_t v; memcpy(&v, in, 4); __atomic_store_n((uint32_t *)dst, v, order); break; }
+   default: { uint64_t v; memcpy(&v, in, 8); __atomic_store_n((uint64_t *)dst, v, order); break; }
+   }
+}
+static void read_bytes(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, void *out, size_t bytes)
+{
+   unsigned char *dst = out;
+   if (small_access(offset, bytes) && (!r->backed || r->backed[offset / UNIT])) {
+      load_small((void *)(r->base + offset), out, bytes, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+      return;
+   }
+   if (vector_access(offset, bytes) && (!r->backed || r->backed[offset / UNIT])) {
+      vec16 v = *(volatile const vec16 *)(r->base + offset);
+      memcpy(out, &v, 16);
+      return;
+   }
+   while (bytes) {
+      size_t part = UNIT - offset % UNIT; if (part > bytes) part = bytes;
+      if (r->backed && !r->backed[offset / UNIT]) memset(dst, 0, part);
+      else memcpy(dst, (void *)(r->base + offset), part);
+      offset += part; dst += part; bytes -= part;
+   }
+}
+static void dirty(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   // Written only when still clear: a store to a page already marked must not write the shared byte again (every thread
+   // storing near it bounced the cache line: 4 threads took 310 ns a store against 20 ns for one).
+   if (!r->dirty || !bytes) return;
+   for (size_t p = offset / LOGICAL, last = (offset + bytes - 1) / LOGICAL; p <= last; p++)
+      if (!__atomic_load_n(&r->dirty[p], __ATOMIC_RELAXED)) __atomic_store_n(&r->dirty[p], 1, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+}
+static int allowed(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes, int bit)
+{
+   if (!span(r, offset, bytes)) return 0;
+   if (!bytes) return 1;
+   size_t end = (offset + bytes - 1) / LOGICAL;
+   for (size_t p = offset / LOGICAL; p <= end; p++) if (!(r->rights[p] & bit)) return 0;
+   return 1;
+}
+static int external_buffer(struct ps5_checked_region *r, const void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!bytes) return 1;
+   uintptr_t at = (uintptr_t)host;
+   if (!host || at > UINTPTR_MAX - bytes) return 0;
+   return at + bytes <= r->base || at >= r->base + r->reserved;
+}
+
+// ---- Gated access (MKWBOOT-038). The caller holds the registry gate shared (ps5_guest_memory.c): protect, commit,
+// decommit, prepare_write, poke/peek, 16-byte atomics and unmap all take that gate exclusively first, so they cannot run
+// meanwhile and the region lock would only repeat it. Up to 16 bytes, host buffer known to be outside the guest. Backing
+// is the one thing a shared holder cannot create: an unbacked unit answers -1 (a store) and the caller takes the slow path.
+int ps5_checked_read_gated(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1)) return 0;
+   if (r->backed && !__atomic_load_n(&r->backed[offset / UNIT], __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE)) { memset(host, 0, bytes); return 1; }
+   if (small_access(offset, bytes)) load_small((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   else if (vector_access(offset, bytes)) { vec16 v = *(volatile const vec16 *)(r->base + offset); memcpy(host, &v, 16); }
+   else read_bytes(r, offset, host, bytes);
+   return 1;
+}
+int ps5_checked_write_gated(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, const void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (!allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2)) return 0;
+   if (r->backed) {
+      if (!__atomic_load_n(&r->backed[offset / UNIT], __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE)) return -1;
+      if ((offset + bytes - 1) / UNIT != offset / UNIT && !__atomic_load_n(&r->backed[(offset + bytes - 1) / UNIT], __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE)) return -1;
+   }
+   if (small_access(offset, bytes)) store_small((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+   else if (vector_access(offset, bytes)) { vec16 v; memcpy(&v, host, 16); *(volatile vec16 *)(r->base + offset) = v; }
+   else memcpy((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes);
+   dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+   return 1;
+}
+int ps5_checked_read(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && external_buffer(r, host, bytes);
+   if (ok && bytes) read_bytes(r, offset, host, bytes);
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_write(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, const void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   // Shared when every touched 64 KiB unit is already backed: data stores need exclusion only from protection and
+   // backing changes, which take the lock exclusively. Racing stores to one address are the guest's own race.
+   // Only up to 16 bytes (a guest scalar or vector store, one host access). Anything larger keeps whole-operation
+   // exclusion, as the contract says: a concurrent reader never sees half of a bulk write.
+   unsigned lt;
+   int ok;
+   if (bytes <= 16) {
+      lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      ok = allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) && external_buffer(r, host, bytes);
+      if (ok && backed_all(r, offset, bytes)) {
+         if (bytes) {
+            if (small_access(offset, bytes)) store_small((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
+            else if (vector_access(offset, bytes)) { vec16 v; memcpy(&v, host, 16); *(volatile vec16 *)(r->base + offset) = v; }
+            else memcpy((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes);
+            dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+         }
+         ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+         return 1;
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+      if (!ok) return 0;
+   }
+   lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   ok = allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) && external_buffer(r, host, bytes) && ensure_backing(r, offset, bytes);
+   if (ok && bytes) { memcpy((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes); dirty(r, offset, bytes); }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return ok;
+}
+/* MKWBOOT-071: 16-byte atomics under the shared lock, with the CPU's own instructions: an aligned 16-byte AVX load is atomic
+ * on the console's CPU (AMD APM: aligned 16-byte accesses are atomic on processors with AVX), and LOCK CMPXCHG16B does the
+ * exchange (it always writes the line, so only on writable, backed pages). The game's lock-free queues use them all the
+ * time; the exclusive lock made every one of them wait for the others (umtx and sched_yield from ps5_checked_atomic_cas, 070). */
+#if defined(__x86_64__)
+#define HAVE_CAS16 1
+typedef unsigned __int128 u128_t;
+static inline int cas16(void *at, u128_t *expected, u128_t desired)
+{
+   uint64_t elo = (uint64_t)*expected, ehi = (uint64_t)(*expected >> 64), dlo = (uint64_t)desired, dhi = (uint64_t)(desired >> 64);
+   unsigned char ok;
+   __asm__ volatile("lock cmpxchg16b %1\n\tsete %0"
+                    : "=q"(ok), "+m"(*(volatile u128_t *)at), "+a"(elo), "+d"(ehi)
+                    : "b"(dlo), "c"(dhi)
+                    : "memory", "cc");
+   *expected = ((u128_t)ehi << 64) | elo;
+   return ok;
+}
+static inline u128_t load16(const void *at)
+{
+   typedef long long v2 __attribute__((vector_size(16)));
+   v2 v;
+   __asm__ volatile("vmovdqa %1, %0" : "=x"(v) : "m"(*(const volatile v2 *)at) : "memory");
+   u128_t r; memcpy(&r, &v, 16); return r;
+}
+#endif
+static int atomic_width(size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   return (bytes == 1 || bytes == 2 || bytes == 4 || bytes == 8 || bytes == 16) && !(offset % bytes);
+}
+int ps5_checked_atomic_load(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, void *out, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (bytes != 16) {   // up to 8 bytes: a host atomic under the shared lock
+      unsigned ft = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      int fast = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && external_buffer(r, out, bytes);
+      if (fast) {
+         if (r->backed && !r->backed[offset / UNIT]) memset(out, 0, bytes);
+         else load_small((void *)(r->base + offset), out, bytes, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+      return fast;
+   }
+#ifdef HAVE_CAS16
+   {
+      unsigned ft = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      int fast = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && external_buffer(r, out, bytes);
+      if (fast) {
+         if (r->backed && !r->backed[offset / UNIT]) memset(out, 0, 16);
+         else { __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST); u128_t v = load16((const void *)(r->base + offset)); memcpy(out, &v, 16); __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST); }
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+      return fast;
+   }
+#endif
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && external_buffer(r, out, bytes);
+   if (ok) {
+      __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+      read_bytes(r, offset, out, bytes);
+      __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_atomic_store(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, const void *in, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (bytes != 16) {
+      unsigned ft = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      int fast = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) && external_buffer(r, in, bytes);
+      if (fast && backed_all(r, offset, bytes)) {
+         store_small((void *)(r->base + offset), in, bytes, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+         dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+         ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+         return 1;
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+      if (!fast) return 0;   // unbacked unit: the exclusive path below commits it
+   }
+#ifdef HAVE_CAS16
+   else {
+      unsigned ft = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      int fast = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) && external_buffer(r, in, bytes);
+      if (fast && backed_all(r, offset, bytes)) {
+         void *at = (void *)(r->base + offset);
+         u128_t want, seen = load16(at);
+         memcpy(&want, in, 16);
+         while (!cas16(at, &seen, want)) {}
+         dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+         ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+         return 1;
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+      if (!fast) return 0;
+   }
+#endif
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) && external_buffer(r, in, bytes) && ensure_backing(r, offset, bytes);
+   if (ok) {
+      __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+      memcpy((void *)(r->base + offset), in, bytes);
+      dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+      __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_atomic_cas(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, void *expected,
+                           const void *desired, size_t bytes, int *exchanged)
+{
+   if (bytes != 16) {
+      unsigned ft = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      int fast = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) &&
+                 external_buffer(r, expected, bytes) && external_buffer(r, desired, bytes) &&
+                 external_buffer(r, exchanged, sizeof *exchanged);
+      if (fast && backed_all(r, offset, bytes)) {
+         void *at = (void *)(r->base + offset);
+         unsigned char exp[8], want[8];
+         memcpy(exp, expected, bytes); memcpy(want, desired, bytes);
+         int equal;
+         switch (bytes) {
+         case 1: { uint8_t e = exp[0], w = want[0]; equal = __atomic_compare_exchange_n((uint8_t *)at, &e, w, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST); exp[0] = e; break; }
+         case 2: { uint16_t e, w; memcpy(&e, exp, 2); memcpy(&w, want, 2); equal = __atomic_compare_exchange_n((uint16_t *)at, &e, w, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST); memcpy(exp, &e, 2); break; }
+         case 4: { uint32_t e, w; memcpy(&e, exp, 4); memcpy(&w, want, 4); equal = __atomic_compare_exchange_n((uint32_t *)at, &e, w, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST); memcpy(exp, &e, 4); break; }
+         default: { uint64_t e, w; memcpy(&e, exp, 8); memcpy(&w, want, 8); equal = __atomic_compare_exchange_n((uint64_t *)at, &e, w, 0, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST, __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST); memcpy(exp, &e, 8); break; }
+         }
+         if (equal) dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+         else memcpy(expected, exp, bytes);   // a failed CAS updates expected, never guest data
+         *exchanged = equal;
+         ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+         return 1;
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+      if (!fast) return 0;
+   }
+#ifdef HAVE_CAS16
+   else {
+      unsigned ft = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+      int fast = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) &&
+                 external_buffer(r, expected, bytes) && external_buffer(r, desired, bytes) &&
+                 external_buffer(r, exchanged, sizeof *exchanged);
+      if (fast && backed_all(r, offset, bytes)) {
+         u128_t exp, want;
+         memcpy(&exp, expected, 16); memcpy(&want, desired, 16);
+         int equal = cas16((void *)(r->base + offset), &exp, want);
+         if (equal) dirty(r, offset, bytes);
+         else memcpy(expected, &exp, 16);   // a failed CAS updates expected, never guest data
+         *exchanged = equal;
+         ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+         return 1;
+      }
+      ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, ft);
+      if (!fast) return 0;
+   }
+#endif
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = atomic_width(offset, bytes) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 1) && allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) &&
+            external_buffer(r, expected, bytes) && external_buffer(r, desired, bytes) &&
+            external_buffer(r, exchanged, sizeof *exchanged);
+   if (ok) {
+      unsigned char want[16], actual[16]; memcpy(want, desired, bytes);
+      __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+      read_bytes(r, offset, actual, bytes);
+      int equal = !memcmp(actual, expected, bytes);
+      if (equal && !ensure_backing(r, offset, bytes)) ok = 0;
+      if (ok) {
+         if (equal) { memcpy((void *)(r->base + offset), want, bytes); dirty(r, offset, bytes); }
+         else memcpy(expected, actual, bytes);
+         __atomic_thread_fence(__ATOMIC_SEQ_CST);
+         *exchanged = equal;
+      }
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_protect(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes, int access)
+{
+   if (offset % LOGICAL || bytes % LOGICAL || (access != 0 && access != 1 && access != 3)) return 0;
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   if (!committed(r, offset, bytes)) { ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return 0; }
+   if (!bytes) { ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return 1; }
+   size_t pages = bytes / LOGICAL, start = offset / LOGICAL;
+   unsigned char *old = malloc(pages);
+   if (!old) { ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return 0; }
+   memcpy(old, r->rights + start, pages);
+   fast_drop(r, start, start + pages, (unsigned char)(access & 3));
+   memset(r->rights + start, access, pages);
+   size_t first = offset / NATIVE, last = (offset + bytes + NATIVE - 1) / NATIVE;
+   int ok = apply(r, first, last);
+   if (!ok) {
+      memcpy(r->rights + start, old, pages);
+      if (!apply(r, first, last)) invalidate(r);
+   }
+   if (!r->poisoned) fast_refresh(r, start, start + pages);
+   free(old);
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_access(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+   int result = !r->poisoned && offset < r->bytes ? r->rights[offset / LOGICAL] : -1;
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return result;
+}
+int ps5_checked_run(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t limit, size_t *end)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+   int result = !r->poisoned && offset < r->bytes && offset < limit ? r->rights[offset / LOGICAL] : -1;
+   size_t at = offset;
+   if (result >= 0) {
+      size_t page = offset / LOGICAL;
+      while (page < r->pages && page * LOGICAL < limit && r->rights[page] == result) page++;
+      at = page * LOGICAL; if (at > limit) at = limit;
+   }
+   if (end) *end = at;
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return result;
+}
+size_t ps5_checked_mixed_pages(struct ps5_checked_region *r)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+   size_t mixed = 0;
+   for (size_t p = 0; p < r->pages; p += 4) {
+      int first = r->rights[p];
+      for (size_t i = p + 1; i < p + 4; i++)
+         if ((i < r->pages ? r->rights[i] : 0) != first) { mixed++; break; }
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt);
+   return mixed;
+}
+uintptr_t ps5_checked_address(const struct ps5_checked_region *r) { return r->base; }
+int ps5_checked_prepare_write(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = allowed(r, offset, bytes, 2) && ensure_backing(r, offset, bytes);
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_commit(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (offset % LOGICAL || bytes % LOGICAL) return 0;
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = r->backed && span(r, offset, bytes);
+   if (ok && bytes) {
+      size_t pages = bytes / LOGICAL, start = offset / LOGICAL;
+      unsigned char *old = malloc(pages);
+      if (!old) ok = 0;
+      else {
+         memcpy(old, r->rights + start, pages);
+         memset(r->rights + start, PS5_CHECKED_RW, pages);
+         size_t first = offset / NATIVE, last = (offset + bytes + NATIVE - 1) / NATIVE;
+         ok = apply(r, first, last);
+         if (!ok) {
+            memcpy(r->rights + start, old, pages);
+            if (!apply(r, first, last)) invalidate(r);
+         }
+         if (!r->poisoned) fast_refresh(r, start, start + pages);
+         free(old);
+      }
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_decommit(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes)
+{
+   if (offset % LOGICAL || bytes % LOGICAL) return 0;
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = r->backed && span(r, offset, bytes);
+   if (ok && bytes) {
+      fast_drop(r, offset / LOGICAL, offset / LOGICAL + bytes / LOGICAL, 0);
+      memset(r->rights + offset / LOGICAL, PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED, bytes / LOGICAL);
+      if (r->dirty) memset(r->dirty + offset / LOGICAL, 0, bytes / LOGICAL);
+      for (size_t u = offset / UNIT; u <= (offset + bytes - 1) / UNIT; u++) {
+         if (!r->backed[u]) continue;
+         size_t low = u * UNIT, high = low + UNIT;
+         int live = 0;
+         for (size_t p = low / LOGICAL; p < high / LOGICAL && p < r->pages; p++)
+            live |= r->rights[p] != PS5_CHECKED_RESERVED;
+         size_t begin = offset > low ? offset : low;
+         size_t end = offset + bytes < high ? offset + bytes : high;
+         int failure = ps5_vrange_decommit((void *)(r->base + (live ? begin : low)), live ? end - begin : UNIT);
+         if (failure) note_failure("ps5_vrange_decommit", r->base + (live ? begin : low), live ? end - begin : UNIT, failure);
+         if (!failure && !live) r->backed[u] = 0;
+         if (failure || !apply(r, low / NATIVE, high / NATIVE)) { invalidate(r); ok = 0; break; }
+      }
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return ok;
+}
+uint64_t ps5_checked_backed_bytes(struct ps5_checked_region *r)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+   uint64_t bytes = 0;
+   if (r->poisoned) bytes = UINT64_MAX;
+   else if (!r->backed) bytes = r->reserved;
+   else for (size_t u = 0; u < r->reserved / UNIT; u++) bytes += r->backed[u] ? UNIT : 0;
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return bytes;
+}
+int ps5_checked_poke(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, const void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = committed(r, offset, bytes) && external_buffer(r, host, bytes) && ensure_backing(r, offset, bytes);
+   if (ok && bytes) {
+      size_t first = offset / NATIVE, last = (offset + bytes + NATIVE - 1) / NATIVE;
+      ok = !sceKernelMprotect((void *)(r->base + first * NATIVE), (last - first) * NATIVE, 3);
+      if (ok) { memcpy((void *)(r->base + offset), host, bytes); dirty(r, offset, bytes); }
+      if (!apply(r, first, last)) { invalidate(r); ok = 0; }
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return ok;
+}
+int ps5_checked_peek(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, void *host, size_t bytes)
+{
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_wrlock(&r->lock);
+   int ok = committed(r, offset, bytes) && external_buffer(r, host, bytes);
+   unsigned char local[256];
+   unsigned char *stage = ok && bytes ? (bytes <= sizeof local ? local : malloc(bytes)) : NULL;
+   if (ok && bytes && !stage) ok = 0;
+   size_t done = 0;
+   while (ok && done < bytes) {
+      size_t at = offset + done, part = UNIT - at % UNIT; if (part > bytes - done) part = bytes - done;
+      if (r->backed && !r->backed[at / UNIT]) memset(stage + done, 0, part);
+      else {
+         size_t first = at / NATIVE, last = (at + part + NATIVE - 1) / NATIVE;
+         ok = !sceKernelMprotect((void *)(r->base + first * NATIVE), (last - first) * NATIVE, 3);
+         if (ok) memcpy(stage + done, (void *)(r->base + at), part);
+         if (!apply(r, first, last)) { invalidate(r); ok = 0; }
+      }
+      done += part;
+   }
+   if (ok && bytes) memcpy(host, stage, bytes);
+   if (stage != local) free(stage);
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return ok;
+}
+uint64_t ps5_checked_written(struct ps5_checked_region *r, size_t offset, size_t bytes,
+                              uint64_t *pages, size_t max, int reset)
+{
+   /* MKWBOOT-092: shared. The dirty bytes are set without the lock by the stores; a reset takes each one with an exchange,
+    * so two queries of the same range at once report every written page exactly once between them. */
+   unsigned lt = ps5_biglock_rdlock(&r->lock);
+   uint64_t count = 0;
+   if (!r->dirty || !span(r, offset, bytes) || max > SIZE_MAX / sizeof(*pages) ||
+       !external_buffer(r, pages, max * sizeof(*pages))) count = UINT64_MAX;
+   else if (bytes) for (size_t p = offset / LOGICAL; p <= (offset + bytes - 1) / LOGICAL && count < max; p++) {
+      if (!__atomic_load_n(&r->dirty[p], __ATOMIC_RELAXED)) continue;
+      if (reset && !__atomic_exchange_n(&r->dirty[p], 0, __ATOMIC_ACQ_REL)) continue;
+      pages[count++] = r->base + p * LOGICAL;
+   }
+   ps5_biglock_unlock(&r->lock, lt); return count;
+}
